# TODO - Universal asset importer

## Objective

Provide one shared import and conversion interface.

## Acceptance

- Identify supported media formats and layouts without requiring a known
  source SHA or checksum inventory. Attempt bounded extraction of supplied
  media and report unsupported structures; preserve all safety checks.
- Remove mandatory original-media digest admission from shared and MAME
  import paths. Do not persist original-media checksum inventories.
- Safely extract and normalise user-provided assets into local storage.
- Validate deterministic output without executing original binaries.
- Support one preferred official edition per title initially.

## Evidence

The shared importer admits a size-bounded immutable source snapshot before
opening the ZIP, with no original-media fingerprint requirement. The flat-file
adapter admits stored and deflated entries
through shared archive budgets, rejects duplicate and case-colliding names,
unsafe paths, unsupported member types and inconsistent ZIP headers. It
checks decoded length and CRC before each conversion callback. Six synthetic
tests pass. The read-only verification CLI also passed on the supplied
Simpsons World set 1 input with exact source, member, total and count limits.
It decoded 14 members and 7,209,088 bytes without extraction or execution.
Synthetic conversion tests cover deterministic manifest ordering, payload
deduplication, output budget enforcement and rejection after source or
staging errors. The staging sink is caller-owned and must discard all
outputs on failure. The Linux adapter verifies the exact content-addressed
payload set and stages the manifest in the same private directory. Native
tests exercise bundle integrity and rejection of missing, extra and wrong
content. Title-specific conversion and publication integration remain open.

GNU/Linux publication now synchronises each unique payload, the manifest
and their staging directories before an atomic no-replace rename into a
manifest-SHA-256-addressed sibling directory. The receipt distinguishes a
successful rename with failed parent-directory sync from a durable
publication. Synthetic tests verify persistence, exact manifest and payload
bytes, and rejection of existing destinations (including a symlink).
Fresh validation passed in run 1795578494631580142-2996234.
Crash fault injection, runtime consumption and Android publication remain
unverified. The publication parent and staging files must remain trusted.

The host composition tool now connects the selected graphics converter
to native transactional staging without introducing an importer-to-platform
dependency. On GNU/Linux the actual supplied Simpsons archive produced a
published manifest-addressed bundle with 10,485,760 payload bytes.
Independent pinned-manifest read-back verified both payloads.
A repeated import rejected the existing destination and left its contents
unchanged. Outputs are retained only beneath the ignored local reference
directory. Other titles, Android publication and full gameplay remain open.

The MAME adapter now selects an explicit machine and supported layout. The
Simpsons machine is a declarative Konami board profile: the shared adapter
collects exact ROM lanes, reuses chip bit layouts, converts each region and
checks the expected manifest shape. The host CLI uses MAME verify, import,
publish and check operations rather than title-specific flags. Local native
round-trip verification reproduced the previously pinned manifest identity
and both payload hashes. The old bundle also passed the new reader.
Only the selected Simpsons profile is verified; other boards remain open.

The same machine selection and transactional publication path now support
a distinct canonical audio family. The KDSC catalogue parser checks every
header and contiguous sample extent before converting one clip at a time.
The supplied Simpsons source produced 161 mono PCM16 records, 3,798,888
payload bytes in total, with declared nominal rates preserved. Two imports
and native published read-back returned the same manifest; repeated
publication rejected the destination and read-back remained unchanged.
At that checkpoint the graphics family retained its manifest identity. A
cross-family read-back was rejected. This verifies conversion, not game audio.

The shared source snapshot and generic ZIP command no longer accept a
pre-known original digest. MAME verify checks every declared graphics
and sample lane by name and size, in addition to shared archive safety
and internal ZIP integrity checks. Imports still enforce exact selected
layout, family-specific media structure and transactional publication.
The checks do not establish original-media authenticity.

Local Simpsons generic and MAME verification each admitted 14 members
and 7,209,088 decoded bytes without a source checksum argument.
Fresh graphics and audio publications produced version-two manifests
with no source fingerprint; independent read-back verified both families.
Their canonical payload records match the existing version-one bundles
exactly. Legacy graphics and audio read-back also retained their original
canonical manifest addresses. No original archive or payload is tracked.

The shared snapshot now also admits bounded NEO version-one cartridge
containers. It validates the 4096-byte header, all six little-endian
region lengths, exact file extent, required program and sprite regions,
and every nonempty region's shared entry and aggregate budgets before
calling the visitor. It borrows region bytes without extracting or
executing them, and does not trust header text as a title identifier.
Synthetic tests cover missing and truncated regions, unknown versions,
trailing data, all three archive budgets, and visitor failure.

Read-only `games-build neo verify` admitted the supplied Metal Slug
container as five regions containing 27,525,120 bytes, with exact source,
entry, aggregate and count limits. A one-byte reduction in the entry
budget rejected the same source. This proves structural admission only:
cartridge-specific graphics/audio conversion, edition authenticity,
and gameplay are not established by the parser.

The NEO adapter now selects the Metal Slug MVS/AES cartridge layout by
exact region sizes, with an explicit revision identifier. Local
comparison against the public Neo Geo board layout found that the
container's two interleaved sprite banks correspond to the original
chip lanes. The alternate ZIP uses a different sprite encoding and is
not admitted by this profile. The shared tile decoder converts both
the Neo Geo fixed layer and sprites without separate payload assembly
or publication logic. No original-media checksum inventory is stored.

Local import, publication and independent pinned read-back produced
262,144 fixed-layer bytes and 33,554,432 sprite index bytes, for
33,816,576 canonical bytes. Repeated publication rejected replacement;
read-back remained unchanged. The existing Simpsons graphics and audio
imports reproduced their previously published manifest identities.
All extracted payloads remain under the ignored reference directory.
Synthetic tests exercise nibble order, sprite bit and half order, exact
region and output budgets, deterministic manifests and identity rejection.
Live palette matching, original scene comparison, audio conversion and
title gameplay remain open.

Complete logical payload budgets are now preflighted by graphics layout
and, for audio, by the admitted sample catalogue before any canonical
payload is decoded or staged. MAME graphics reject insufficient output
budgets before archive extraction; NEO graphics use the same policy.
Preflight and actual writes share per-record, individual-size and
aggregate-size admission. Tests reject undersized budgets before staging
and preserve acceptance at exact limits. Encoded manifest size is still
checked at completion by the canonical transaction, whose private sink
must be discarded after any error.

The unified staged-tree check preserved all three locally verified
conversion results: Simpsons graphics (10,485,760 bytes), Simpsons
audio (161 unique payloads, 3,798,888 bytes), and Metal Slug graphics
(33,816,576 bytes). Fresh optimized imports, pinned read-back and
byte-for-byte comparisons matched the existing canonical bundles.
Repeated graphics publication failed with a collision; subsequent
read-back remained unchanged. All output stayed under ignored local
reference directories, with no surviving temporary staging directory.
